hexagon: send a pointwise unary op on contiguous tensors with long rows
Fold-into: upstream/hexagon-host

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus every patch of the series before this one.

The defect

The unary kernel of the DSP (htp/unary-ops.c) starts one DMA row and one HVX
call for each row of src0. Thus rows of less than 512 bytes make the op slow,
and a tensor that has a smaller number of rows than threads keeps some threads
without work. Three ops of Qwen3.5 (src/models/qwen35.cpp, and build_rs of
src/llama-graph.cpp) have such shapes. On the phone (the 4B Q8_0 on HTP0, a
prompt ubatch of 1024 tokens):

- The beta gate of each gated delta net layer is a SIGMOID of the shape
  [1, 32, 1024], thus each row holds one element. The 24 ops took 10.4 ms, at
  0.6 GB/s.
- The SOFTPLUS of the gate has rows of 32 elements (128 bytes). The 24 ops took
  1.9 ms, at 3 GB/s.
- In the first ubatch of a sequence, a SCALE by 0 sets the recurrent state of
  each layer to zero. The state of a gated delta net layer is one row of 2 MiB,
  and one thread moved it in 115 us (24 ops, 2.8 ms).

The change

A pointwise op gives the same value for each element with each row length. The
pointwise ops are SCALE, CLAMP, LEAKY_RELU, SQR, SQRT and the unary ops NEG,
EXP, SIGMOID, SILU, GELU, GELU_QUICK, SOFTPLUS, TANH, ABS, LOG and RELU.
ggml_hexagon_flatten_pointwise gives such an op new descriptors of src0 and dst
when the two are contiguous, have the same type and the same element count, and
when one of these conditions is true:

- The rows are shorter than 512 bytes.
- The rows are longer than 16 KiB, and the tensor has a smaller number of rows
  than threads.

The rows of the new descriptors have the largest length that divides the
element count and holds a maximum of 16 KiB. The op node keeps the two
descriptors as copies of the tensors (htp_opnode::add_dummy), with the data,
the buffer and the extra of each tensor. The kernel params come from the new
shape. The attention gate (a SIGMOID with rows of 4096 elements) and the norms
keep their rows.

The op reads and writes the same bytes, and each HVX lane computes one element.
But the new rows put an element into a different block of the kernel: the
SOFTPLUS computes a group of 4 vectors in its main block and the remaining
vectors in its tail block, and the int16 SIGMOID converts two vectors to f16
together. In the simulator, with the flags of the shipped library, the old and
the new rows give the same bits. On the NPU, a small number of elements round
differently (refer to The phone). The two forms have the same error against the
CPU, thus the difference is an intended difference of the arithmetic.

The switch GGML_HEXAGON_UNARY_FLAT (preset 1) gives 0: no op gets new rows. A
different value is a mask of the kinds of op: 2 SIGMOID, 4 SOFTPLUS, 8 SCALE
and 16 the other pointwise ops.

The tests

The symbolic check of tests/fuzz/hexhost (common/symexec.cpp) gives a pointwise
op a value of its element count and not of its shape. Without that rule, the
test mode gives 12 findings of sym-mismatch at the new descriptors. With it, the
test mode gives 0 findings in the configurations none, asan, ubsan, tsan and
msan, each with the debug and the release profile, and the graphs mode passes
on the 2B and the 4B. On the fake DSP, each value of the switch changes the
rows of the kinds that it names and of no other op.

The phone

The stage unary-rows (tools/stages/unary-rows, the 4B Q8_0 on HTP0) compared
the old and the new rows:

- With the value 0, the logits hashes are those of the series before this
  patch. Of the three kinds, only the new rows of the SOFTPLUS move the hashes
  of the 4B. On the 2B, the SIGMOID and the SCALE (the value 26) also move the
  hashes.
- unarycheck ran the ops in the shapes of the model on HTP0 and on the CPU of
  the phone. The old and the new rows have the same error against the CPU: the
  SIGMOID [1, 32, 1024] has an NMSE of 1.725e-9 with the two forms, and the
  SOFTPLUS [32, 1024] has 2.460e-16 and 2.459e-16. Between the two forms, 167 of
  32768 SOFTPLUS elements differ by 1 ulp, and 4 of 32768 SIGMOID elements by a
  maximum of 9.2e-5 (one f16 step of the input). The SCALE by 0 clears its slot
  with the two forms, and the decode shapes give the same bits.

The stage unary-kl (tools/stages/unary-kl) measured the KL against the naive
base naive-4B-q8.kld, with each variant two times:

- Prefill (-b 512, 4 chunks of 512): 0.000579 +- 0.000039 before this patch
  (maximum 0.019190, same top p 98.529 %), 0.000574 +- 0.000038 with it
  (maximum 0.018415, same top p 98.529 %).
- Decode (-b 1 -ub 1, 1 chunk of 512): 0.000557 +- 0.000089 with the two forms.
  The decode logits have the same bits.

llama-bench against the series before this patch, the medians of 3 alternated
rounds: pp512 +2.46 % (range +0.78 to +3.02), pp1024 +0.66 % (+0.15 to
+0.92), and tg32 at the depth 0 -0.25 % (-0.77 to -0.23). The value 26 runs the
same ops as this patch in decode, and it gives -0.41 % (-0.62 to +0.49) in the
same rounds, thus the spread of the tg32 runs is more than 1 %. The profile of
a prompt ubatch of 1024 tokens (the stage quick) gives the SIGMOID 10.37 ms to
0.41 ms, the SOFTPLUS 1.90 ms to 0.93 ms and the clear SCALE 2.76 ms to 2.00 ms.

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/ggml-hexagon.cpp b/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/ggml-hexagon.cpp
index 898e50d..4a17422 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/ggml-hexagon.cpp
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/ggml-hexagon.cpp
@@ -163,6 +163,11 @@ static int opt_mm_solver = 1;
 static int opt_mm_chunks_m = -1;
 static int opt_mm_chunks_n = -1;
 
+// GGML_HEXAGON_UNARY_FLAT: 1 = ggml_hexagon_flatten_pointwise gives new rows to each kind of pointwise op
+// (the default), 0 = to no op. A different value is a mask of the kinds: 2 = SIGMOID, 4 = SOFTPLUS,
+// 8 = SCALE, 16 = the other pointwise ops. Thus an A/B run of each kind needs no other build.
+static int opt_unary_flat = 1;
+
 static inline bool ggml_hexagon_is_fusion_enabled(int flag) {
     if (opt_opfusion <= 0) return false;
     if (opt_opfusion == 1) return true; // 1 enables all
@@ -5629,6 +5634,114 @@ static void ggml_hexagon_precompute_unary_params(
     kparams->div_tpr   = init_fastdiv_values(tiles_per_row);
 }
 
+// A row of less than this many bytes makes the unary kernel slow. The phone measured 3 GB/s for rows of
+// 128 bytes and 58 GB/s for rows of 512 bytes.
+static constexpr size_t HTP_UNARY_SHORT_ROW_BYTES = 512;
+
+// The maximum bytes of one row that ggml_hexagon_flatten_pointwise makes. With 6 threads, 8 MiB of VTCM
+// holds 21 such rows of src0 and of dst for each thread, in two halves.
+static constexpr size_t HTP_UNARY_FLAT_ROW_BYTES = 16384;
+
+// True for a unary opcode whose value at each element uses only the source value at that element
+static bool ggml_hexagon_op_is_pointwise(uint32_t opcode) {
+    switch (opcode) {
+        case HTP_OP_SCALE:
+        case HTP_OP_CLAMP:
+        case HTP_OP_LEAKY_RELU:
+        case HTP_OP_SQR:
+        case HTP_OP_SQRT:
+        case HTP_OP_UNARY_NEG:
+        case HTP_OP_UNARY_EXP:
+        case HTP_OP_UNARY_SIGMOID:
+        case HTP_OP_UNARY_SILU:
+        case HTP_OP_UNARY_GELU:
+        case HTP_OP_UNARY_GELU_QUICK:
+        case HTP_OP_UNARY_SOFTPLUS:
+        case HTP_OP_UNARY_TANH:
+        case HTP_OP_UNARY_ABS:
+        case HTP_OP_UNARY_LOG:
+        case HTP_OP_UNARY_RELU:
+            return true;
+        default:
+            return false;
+    }
+}
+
+// True when GGML_HEXAGON_UNARY_FLAT includes the kind of this pointwise opcode
+static bool ggml_hexagon_unary_flat_enabled(uint32_t opcode) {
+    if (opt_unary_flat <= 0) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    if (opt_unary_flat == 1) {
+        return true;
+    }
+    const int kind = opcode == HTP_OP_UNARY_SIGMOID  ? 2 :
+                     opcode == HTP_OP_UNARY_SOFTPLUS ? 4 :
+                     opcode == HTP_OP_SCALE          ? 8 : 16;
+    return (opt_unary_flat & kind) != 0;
+}
+
+// The unary kernel of the DSP (htp/unary-ops.c) starts one DMA row and one HVX call for each row of
+// src0. Thus rows of less than HTP_UNARY_SHORT_ROW_BYTES make the op slow. On the phone, the beta gate of
+// a gated delta net layer has rows of one element, and it moved 0.6 GB/s. A tensor that has a smaller
+// number of rows than threads keeps some threads without work. The SCALE by 0 that sets a recurrent
+// state to zero (build_rs of llama-graph.cpp) is one row of 2 MiB, and one thread moves it.
+//
+// A pointwise op on contiguous tensors gives the same value for each element with each row length. Thus
+// the host gives such an op new descriptors of src0 and dst. Their rows have the largest length that
+// divides the element count and holds a maximum of HTP_UNARY_FLAT_ROW_BYTES. The op node keeps the two
+// descriptors as copies of the tensors (htp_opnode::add_dummy), with the data, the buffer and the extra of
+// each tensor. When the rows of the tensor are short, the rows of the copies are not shorter, because ne0
+// divides the element count. O(HTP_UNARY_FLAT_ROW_BYTES / element size) when the host makes the op node.
+static void ggml_hexagon_flatten_pointwise(const struct ggml_hexagon_session * sess, htp_opnode & node) {
+    if (!ggml_hexagon_op_is_pointwise(node.opcode) || !ggml_hexagon_unary_flat_enabled(node.opcode) ||
+        !node.fused.empty() || node.inputs.size() != 1 || node.outputs.size() != 1 || node.inputs[0] == nullptr) {
+        return;
+    }
+
+    const ggml_tensor * src0 = node.inputs[0];
+    const ggml_tensor * dst  = node.outputs[0];
+    if (src0->type != dst->type || ggml_blck_size(src0->type) != 1 || !ggml_is_contiguous(src0) ||
+        !ggml_is_contiguous(dst) || ggml_nelements(src0) != ggml_nelements(dst) || ggml_nelements(src0) <= 0) {
+        return;
+    }
+
+    const int64_t n    = ggml_nelements(src0);
+    const size_t  es   = ggml_type_size(src0->type);
+    const int64_t ne0  = src0->ne[0];
+    const int64_t rows = n / ne0;
+
+    const bool short_rows = (size_t) ne0 * es < HTP_UNARY_SHORT_ROW_BYTES;
+    const bool long_rows  = (size_t) ne0 * es > HTP_UNARY_FLAT_ROW_BYTES;
+    if (!short_rows && !(long_rows && rows < (int64_t) sess->n_threads)) {
+        return;
+    }
+
+    int64_t len = std::min<int64_t>(n, (int64_t) (HTP_UNARY_FLAT_ROW_BYTES / es));
+    while (n % len != 0) {
+        len--;
+    }
+    if (len == ne0) {
+        return;
+    }
+
+    auto flat = [&](const ggml_tensor * t) {
+        ggml_tensor c = *t;
+        c.ne[0] = len;
+        c.ne[1] = n / len;
+        c.ne[2] = 1;
+        c.ne[3] = 1;
+        c.nb[0] = es;
+        c.nb[1] = (size_t) len * es;
+        c.nb[2] = (size_t) n * es;
+        c.nb[3] = (size_t) n * es;
+        return node.add_dummy(c);
+    };
+
+    node.inputs[0]  = flat(src0);
+    node.outputs[0] = flat(dst);
+}
+
 static void ggml_hexagon_precompute_get_rows_params(
     const struct ggml_hexagon_session * sess,
     const struct ggml_tensor * src0,
@@ -7128,6 +7241,7 @@ static ggml_status ggml_backend_hexagon_graph_compute(ggml_backend_t backend, gg
                     (struct htp_fa_kernel_params *)node.kernel_params
                 );
             } else if (htp_op_is_unary(node.opcode)) {
+                ggml_hexagon_flatten_pointwise(sess, node);
                 auto inputs = node.get_inputs();
                 const struct ggml_tensor * src0 = inputs[0];
                 const struct ggml_tensor * src1 = inputs.size() > 1 ? inputs[1] : nullptr;
@@ -8568,6 +8682,7 @@ static void ggml_hexagon_init(ggml_backend_reg * reg) {
